const body = `<h1>4.2b — for Loops: Item Lister & Stat Printer</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        for loops excel at two things: repeating an action a known number of times,
        and walking through a range of numbers. Today you build RPG tools that do both.
    </p>

    <h3>Build 1: Inventory Printer</h3>

    <p>Create <code>ItemLister.java</code>. Given an array of item names, print a numbered inventory:</p>
    <pre><code>String[] items = {"Sword", "Potion", "Shield", "Key", "Torch"};

// Output:
═══ INVENTORY ═══
1. Sword
2. Potion
3. Shield
4. Key
5. Torch
6 slots used, 10 total</code></pre>

    <p>The array has 5 items, but the inventory has 10 slots. Print all 10 rows — items 6-10 should say "(empty)". You need a for loop that goes from 0 to 9, and decides what to print based on whether the index has a corresponding item.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The ArrayIndexOutOfBounds</h3>
        <p>
            If you write <code>for (int i = 0; i <= 10; i++)</code>, what happens
            when i = 5 and you access items[5]? Why does the loop keep going to 10
            if the array only has 5 elements?
        </p>
        <p>
            The fix: check <code>if (i < items.length)</code> before accessing the
            array. This is called <strong>bounds checking</strong> — and it's how
            every professional programmer avoids this crash.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Build 2: Multi-Table Roller</h3>

    <p>Create <code>LootRoller.java</code>. A loot table has multiple items with different drop rates. Roll for each one:</p>
    <pre><code>String[] table = {"Gold (50%)", "Potion (30%)", "Sword (10%)", "Armor (5%)", "Dragon Egg (1%)"};
double[] rates = {0.50, 0.30, 0.10, 0.05, 0.01};

System.out.println("Rolling loot...");
for (int i = 0; i < table.length; i++) {
    double roll = Math.random();
    if (roll < rates[i]) {
        System.out.println("★★★ " + table[i] + " — DROPPED!");
    }
}</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why does <code>Math.random() < 0.50</code> give a 50% chance? If you wanted 75%, what number would you compare to?</li>
            <li>If you run LootRoller 10 times, will you always get the Dragon Egg at least once? Is there a guarantee? (Think: 1% chance, 10 trials...)</li>
            <li>What's the relationship between the for loop counter <code>i</code> and the tables array? Why must they be the same length?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Use for loops to iterate over arrays with bounds checking</li>
            <li>Combine Math.random() with a loop to roll multiple probabilities</li>
            <li>Print formatted numbered lists with conditional logic inside the loop</li>
            <li>Explain why the loop counter and array index are the same variable</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/for-loops.html">for Loops</a> — syntax, scope, off-by-one</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/array-basics.html">Arrays</a> — .length, index access</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/math-class.html">The Math Class</a> — random()</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
